arXiv:2510.04436v1 [cs.RO] 06 Oct 2025</div>
</div><div class="ltx_page_content">
<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_document" style="font-size:173%;">PAD-TRO: Projection-Augmented Diffusion for Direct Trajectory Optimization</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Jushan Chen
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Santiago Paternain
</span><span id="id1" class="ltx_note ltx_note_frontmatter ltx_thanks_note ltx_role_thanks"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">thanks: </span>The authors are with the Department of Electrical, Computer, and Systems Engineering, Rensselaer Polytechnic Institute, 110 8th St, Troy, NY 12180, USA. <span id="id1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;">{chenj72, paters}@rpi.edu</span></span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Recently, diffusion models have gained popularity and attention in trajectory optimization due to their capability of modeling multi-modal probability distributions. However, addressing nonlinear equality constraints, i.e, dynamic feasibility, remains a great challenge in diffusion-based trajectory optimization. Recent diffusion-based trajectory optimization frameworks rely on a single-shooting style approach where the denoised control sequence is applied to forward propagate the dynamical system, which cannot explicitly enforce constraints on the states and frequently leads to sub-optimal solutions. In this work, we propose a novel direct trajectory optimization approach via model-based diffusion, which directly generates a sequence of states. To ensure dynamic feasibility, we propose a gradient-free projection mechanism that is incorporated into the reverse diffusion process. Our results show that, compared to a recent state-of-the-art baseline, our approach leads to zero dynamic feasibility error and approximately 4x higher success rate in a quadrotor waypoint navigation scenario involving dense static obstacles.</p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">I </span><span id="S1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">INTRODUCTION</span></h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Trajectory optimization is a critical task in robotics. Traditional methods in trajectory optimization transcribe the original continuous-time optimization problem into a corresponding nonlinear programming problem (NLP) via shooting methods or collocation methods. In particular, shooting-based methods use the control variables as optimization variables and enforce dynamic feasibility via forward propagation, whereas collocation methods are entirely based on function approximation and use both control and state variables as the optimization variables <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>. Solving such NLPs requires gradient-based optimization <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite> such as interior-point solvers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>, which are often not robust against nonconvex objectives or constraints and might be stuck in local minima. In contrast, sampling based trajectory optimization methods are, in general, more robust to local minima, such as the Model Predictive Path Integral <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite> framework and Cross Entropy motion planning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>, but the downside is that the quality of solutions is highly dependent on the scale of Gaussian noise, which might lead to over-approximation or under-approximation. On the other hand, geometric path planning algorithms such as RRT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite> and the many variants that follow from RRT, are efficient at finding a global path, but they disregard the problem-specific constraints and dynamic feasibility for robotic systems.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Recently, diffusion models have been widely adopted in computer vision tasks such as image and video generation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>, <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>. Due to its expressive power of modeling multi-modal distributions, several recent works have incorporated diffusion in trajectory optimization or control policy learning in robotics <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>, <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>, <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>, <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>. In particular, these diffusion-style methods can be divided into two categories: learning-based and model-based. Learning-based trajectory planning frameworks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>, <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite> rely on expert demonstration data to train a noise prediction network. Then, trajectory generation is performed in a reverse diffusion process. They resemble the classic Denoising Diffusion Probabilistic Models (DDPM) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>. In contrast, model-based diffusion frameworks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>, <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>, <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite> assume that we have some knowledge about the target distribution of optimal trajectories, and rely on Langevin sampling while estimating the score function, i.e., the gradient of the log-likelihood. For example, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>, <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>, <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite> assume that the target distribution of the optimal trajectories follows a Boltzmann distribution. Intuitively, the cost function is optimized by sampling from the corresponding Boltzmann distribution by annealing the temperature coefficient to zero <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>. Model-Based Diffusion (MBD) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite> solves the trajectory optimization problem by generating a sequence of control actions at each step of the reverse diffusion process. Then, the sequence of control actions in the last reverse diffusion step is applied to forward propagate the system dynamics, leading to a sequence of dynamically feasible states. In other words, MBD <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite> is analogous to single shooting <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite> in classic trajectory optimization. In contrast, Equality Constrained Diffusion for Direct Trajectory Optimization (DRAX) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite> generates both a sequence of states and actions via diffusion, and enforces soft dynamic feasibility by using an augmented Lagrangian term to penalize violation of dynamic feasibility. To the best of the authors’ knowledge, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite> is the only recent work that attempts to address nonlinear equality constraints in a model-based diffusion framework for direct trajectory optimization.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">However, both MBD <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite> and DRAX <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite> suffer from critical drawbacks: we empirically discover that for a high-dimensional dynamical system (e.g., a quadrotor) navigating through a cluttered environment, MBD <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite> cannot achieve close convergence to the goal position (see Section <a href="#S5" title="V Simulation Studies ‣ PAD-TRO: Projection-Augmented Diffusion for Direct Trajectory Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">V</span></a>). On the other hand, DRAX is more robust to local minima. However, due to the soft penalty on the dynamic feasibility violation, the optimized trajectory leads to a high dynamic feasibility violation, potentially inhibiting a low-level controller from trajectory tracking. To address the aforementioned limitations, we propose PAD-TRO, <span id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_underline ltx_font_italic">P</span><span id="S1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">rojection-<span id="S1.p3.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">A</span>ugmented <span id="S1.p3.1.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">D</span>iffusion for Direct <span id="S1.p3.1.2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">Tr</span>ajectory <span id="S1.p3.1.2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">O</span>ptimization</span>. The main contributions of our work are summarized as follows:</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.ix1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(i)</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We propose a novel model-based diffusion algorithm for direct trajectory optimization;</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.ix2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(ii)</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We integrate a gradient-free projection mechanism to address nonlinear equality constraints (i.e., dynamic feasibility) during the reverse diffusion process;</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.ix3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(iii)</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.ix3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.ix3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We show that our method leads to exact convergence to the goal position and results in <span id="S1.I1.ix3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">zero dynamic feasibility violation</span> and <span id="S1.I1.ix3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">4x higher success rate</span> compared to a similar baseline <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">The rest of the work is organized as follows: in Section <a href="#S2" title="II PROBLEM FORMULATION ‣ PAD-TRO: Projection-Augmented Diffusion for Direct Trajectory Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">II</span></a>, we introduce the trajectory optimization problem, which makes explicit the challenges associated with solving it through sampling. Namely, ensuring dynamic feasibility. In Section <a href="#S3" title="III Background on diffusion for Trajectory Optimization ‣ PAD-TRO: Projection-Augmented Diffusion for Direct Trajectory Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">III</span></a>, we discuss the theoretical background relevant to diffusion and trajectory optimization. Then, we present our novel diffusion-based trajectory optimization framework (see Section <a href="#S4" title="IV CONSTRAINED DIFFUSION FOR DIRECT TRAJECTORY OPTIMIZATION ‣ PAD-TRO: Projection-Augmented Diffusion for Direct Trajectory Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">IV</span></a>). Other than concluding remarks in Section <a href="#S6" title="VI Conclusion ‣ PAD-TRO: Projection-Augmented Diffusion for Direct Trajectory Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">VI</span></a>, this work closes with numerical examples where we evaluate the proposed method in a quadrotor navigation scenario in a cluttered environment (see Section <a href="#S5" title="V Simulation Studies ‣ PAD-TRO: Projection-Augmented Diffusion for Direct Trajectory Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">V</span></a>).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">II </span><span id="S2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PROBLEM FORMULATION</span></h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We denote the state and the input of a system as <math id="S2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\mathbb{R}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\mathbb{R}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\in\mathbb{R}^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\in\mathbb{R}^{m}</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively. We further denote the discrete time by subscripts <math id="S2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in\mathbb{N}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℕ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in\mathbb{N}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e., <math id="S2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> refer to the state and input at time <math id="S2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively. We also define for notational convenience <math id="S2.p1.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="x_{1:T}=\left\{x_{t}\right\}_{t=1,\ldots,T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo>{</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>}</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1:T}=\left\{x_{t}\right\}_{t=1,\ldots,T}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p1.m8" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="u_{0:T-1}=~\left\{u_{t}\right\}_{t=0,\ldots,T-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.608em">=</mo><msub><mrow><mo>{</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>}</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{0:T-1}=~\left\{u_{t}\right\}_{t=0,\ldots,T-1}</annotation></semantics></math>. In addition, we assume that <math id="S2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{L}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>U</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{U}</annotation></semantics></math> are the lower and upper bounds of the control input, respectively, and similarly, <math id="S2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{L}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>U</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{U}</annotation></semantics></math> refer to the lower and upper bounds of the state.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:\mathbb{R}^{n}\times\mathbb{R}^{m}\to\mathbb{R}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:\mathbb{R}^{n}\times\mathbb{R}^{m}\to\mathbb{R}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> describe the nonlinear dynamics of the system as a function of the state <math id="S2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> and control input <math id="S2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math>. We assume there are <math id="S2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{obs}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{obs}</annotation></semantics></math> obstacles and denote <math id="S2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g:\mathbb{R}^{n}\to\mathbb{R}^{N_{obs}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>N</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g:\mathbb{R}^{n}\to\mathbb{R}^{N_{obs}}</annotation></semantics></math> as the collision avoidance constraints of the system as a function of <math id="S2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>. <math id="S2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> can represent, for example, a function related to the distance to an obstacle. We also define a terminal set <math id="S2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}_{T}\in\mathbb{R}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}_{T}\in\mathbb{R}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> that constrains the state variable at time <math id="S2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>. Further, denote <math id="S2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J:~\mathbb{R}^{n}\times\mathbb{R}^{m}\to\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.608em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J:~\mathbb{R}^{n}\times\mathbb{R}^{m}\to\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> as the cost function to be minimized, e.g., a quadratic trajectory tracking cost. With these definitions, a general trajectory optimization problem in discrete time can be written as</p>
<table id="S2.E1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S2.E1X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E1X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\min_{\,x_{1:T},\,u_{0:T-1}}\;J(x_{1:T},u_{0:T-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>min</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mi>u</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></msub><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\min_{\,x_{1:T},\,u_{0:T-1}}\;J(x_{1:T},u_{0:T-1})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="6" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S2.E1Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E1Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\text{s.t.}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext>s.t.</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{s.t.}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E1Xa.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle x_{t+1}=f(x_{t},u_{t}),\quad t=0,\dots,T-1," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle x_{t+1}=f(x_{t},u_{t}),\quad t=0,\dots,T-1,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="S2.E1Xb" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E1Xb.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle g(x_{t})\leq 0,\quad t=1,\dots,T," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>≤</mo><mn>0</mn><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle g(x_{t})\leq 0,\quad t=1,\dots,T,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="S2.E1Xc" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E1Xc.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle u_{L}\leq u_{t}\leq u_{U},t=0,\dots,T-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>L</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi>u</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi>u</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>U</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle u_{L}\leq u_{t}\leq u_{U},t=0,\dots,T-1</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="S2.E1Xd" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E1Xd.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle x_{L}\leq x_{t}\leq x_{U},t=1,\dots,T," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>L</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>U</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle x_{L}\leq x_{t}\leq x_{U},t=1,\dots,T,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="S2.E1Xe" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E1Xe.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle x_{T}\in\mathcal{X}_{T}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle x_{T}\in\mathcal{X}_{T},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p2.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\leq" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>≤</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\leq</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\geq" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>≥</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\geq</annotation></semantics></math> denote component-wise inequalities. Note that, since <math id="S2.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is the given initial condition, it is excluded from the decision variables. In sampling-based optimization, we transcribe the trajectory optimization problem in (<a href="#S2.E1" title="Equation 1 ‣ II PROBLEM FORMULATION ‣ PAD-TRO: Projection-Augmented Diffusion for Direct Trajectory Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) into a probability sampling problem <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>. We use lowercase letters to denote real-valued variables, and by a slight abuse of notation, we use uppercase letters to denote corresponding random variables or a batch of real-valued samples. We clarify what uppercase letters refer to whenever necessary.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">While trajectory optimization has a longstanding tradition in solving (<a href="#S2.E1" title="Equation 1 ‣ II PROBLEM FORMULATION ‣ PAD-TRO: Projection-Augmented Diffusion for Direct Trajectory Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) using a nonlinear optimization solver, e.g., Casadi <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>, it does not always results in an optimal or even feasible solution. Thus, to tackle these challenges, we aim to sample a <span id="S2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">target trajectory distribution</span> <math id="S2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> which is given by</p>
<table id="S2.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{0}(X)\propto p_{d}(X)p_{J}(X)p_{g}(X)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∝</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>J</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{0}(X)\propto p_{d}(X)p_{J}(X)p_{g}(X),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{d}(X)=\prod_{t=1}^{T}\mathbf{1}(x_{t}=f(x_{t-1},u_{t-1}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mrow><mn>𝟏</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{d}(X)=\prod_{t=1}^{T}\mathbf{1}(x_{t}=f(x_{t-1},u_{t-1}))</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the dynamic feasibility condition, <math id="S2.p3.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="p_{J}(X)\propto e^{-J(X)/\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>J</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∝</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>/</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{J}(X)\propto e^{-J(X)/\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes optimality of the trajectory, and <math id="S2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{g}(X)\propto\prod_{t=1}^{T}\mathbf{1}\left(g_{t}(x_{t},u_{t})\leq 0\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">∝</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mrow><mn>𝟏</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{g}(X)\propto\prod_{t=1}^{T}\mathbf{1}\left(g_{t}(x_{t},u_{t})\leq 0\right)</annotation></semantics></math> denotes inequality constraints satisfaction. The indicator function in <math id="S2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{d}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{d}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{g}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{g}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> enforce the dynamic feasibility and obstacle avoidance of the trajectory. Indeed, the indicator function evaluates to 1 if the underlying constraints are satisfied; otherwise, it evaluates to 0. The cost of the trajectory <math id="S2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math> is transformed into a Boltzmann distribution <math id="S2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{J}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>J</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{J}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> such that when the exponent is small, we get a value close to 1. The challenge of directly sampling from (<a href="#S2.E2" title="Equation 2 ‣ II PROBLEM FORMULATION ‣ PAD-TRO: Projection-Augmented Diffusion for Direct Trajectory Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) arises from the high-dimensionality and sparsity of samples that strictly follow the target distribution <math id="S2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{0}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{0}(X)</annotation></semantics></math>. In particular, the dynamic constraints have zero measure. To tackle this challenge, our approach combines model-based diffusion with a gradient-free projection on an approximate reachable set. Before describing the algorithm in detail in Section <a href="#S4" title="IV CONSTRAINED DIFFUSION FOR DIRECT TRAJECTORY OPTIMIZATION ‣ PAD-TRO: Projection-Augmented Diffusion for Direct Trajectory Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">IV</span></a>, we discuss the relevant theoretical background of diffusion in the following section.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">III </span><span id="S3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Background on diffusion for Trajectory Optimization</span></h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Diffusion models originate from the theory of Langevin sampling <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>, which aims to sample from a generic probability distribution <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(Z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(Z)</annotation></semantics></math> using the gradient of the log-likelihood function <math id="S3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{z}\log p(Z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>z</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{z}\log p(Z)</annotation></semantics></math>, also known as the score function.
By convention, the standard Langevin Monte Carlo solves an <span id="S3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">unconstrained optimization</span> problem by generating samples from some <span id="S3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">target distribution</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite></p>
<table id="S3.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(Z)\propto e^{-J(Z)}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∝</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(Z)\propto e^{-J(Z)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">via the following Langevin stochastic differential equation (SDE)</p>
<table id="S3.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="dZ=-\nabla_{z}J(Z)+\sqrt{2}dW," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">−</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>z</mi></msub><mi>J</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">dZ=-\nabla_{z}J(Z)+\sqrt{2}dW,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p1.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> denotes a Wiener process random variable, and the score function <math id="S3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{z}\log p(Z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>z</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{z}\log p(Z)</annotation></semantics></math> is equivalent to <math id="S3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-\nabla_{z}J(Z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">−</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>z</mi></msub><mi>J</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-\nabla_{z}J(Z)</annotation></semantics></math>. In this scenario, the Langevin Dynamics are equivalent to stochastic gradient descent. Intuitively, the score function acts as a guidance term such that the diffusion process gradually pushes samples to regions of low cost.
As discussed in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>, existing literature commonly applies the following discrete-time approximation to simulate continuous-time Langevin Dynamics, leading to Stochastic Gradient Langevin Dynamics (SGLD):</p>
<table id="S3.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{i-1}=z_{i}+\frac{\beta_{i}}{2}\nabla_{z}\log p(z_{i})+\sqrt{\beta_{i}}\epsilon,\epsilon\sim\mathcal{N}(0,I)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>z</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msqrt><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{i-1}=z_{i}+\frac{\beta_{i}}{2}\nabla_{z}\log p(z_{i})+\sqrt{\beta_{i}}\epsilon,\epsilon\sim\mathcal{N}(0,I),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p1.4" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is a pre-determined variance schedule. However, applying (<a href="#S3.E5" title="Equation 5 ‣ III Background on diffusion for Trajectory Optimization ‣ PAD-TRO: Projection-Augmented Diffusion for Direct Trajectory Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>) directly to solve the constrained trajectory optimization problem by generating samples from the target distribution <math id="S3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{0}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{0}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S2.E2" title="Equation 2 ‣ II PROBLEM FORMULATION ‣ PAD-TRO: Projection-Augmented Diffusion for Direct Trajectory Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) is impractical due to the composition of dynamic feasibility <math id="S3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{d}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{d}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> and constraints satisfaction <math id="S3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{g}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{g}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math>. To directly compute the score function is challenging unless the objective has specific forms, such as the Boltzmann distribution <math id="S3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{J}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>J</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{J}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla\log p_{J}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>log</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>J</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla\log p_{J}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> only depends
on the gradient of <math id="S3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math>. In particular, the challenge arises in (<a href="#S2.E2" title="Equation 2 ‣ II PROBLEM FORMULATION ‣ PAD-TRO: Projection-Augmented Diffusion for Direct Trajectory Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) where the gradient of <math id="S3.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{d}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{d}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{g}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{g}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> are not well-defined. Consider the dynamic feasibility component, <math id="S3.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla\log p_{d}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>log</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla\log p_{d}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math>: <math id="S3.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{d}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{d}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> evaluates to <math id="S3.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> when the constraint is violated. Then, <math id="S3.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log p_{d}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log p_{d}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> becomes undefined, and thus we have no access to the gradient <math id="S3.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla\log p_{d}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>log</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla\log p_{d}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus, an estimation of the score function for (<a href="#S2.E2" title="Equation 2 ‣ II PROBLEM FORMULATION ‣ PAD-TRO: Projection-Augmented Diffusion for Direct Trajectory Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) is needed. To tackle this challenge, the authors of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite> proposed a model-based diffusion framework for trajectory optimization through the following variance-preserved reverse diffusion process, also known as the “multi-step score ascent”,</p>
<table id="S3.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u^{i-1}=\frac{1}{\sqrt{\alpha_{i}}}(u^{i}+(1-\bar{\alpha}_{i})\nabla_{u^{i}}\log p_{i}(u^{i}))," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msqrt><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msqrt></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mo>∇</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u^{i-1}=\frac{1}{\sqrt{\alpha_{i}}}(u^{i}+(1-\bar{\alpha}_{i})\nabla_{u^{i}}\log p_{i}(u^{i})),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p1.5" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u^{i}</annotation></semantics></math> := <math id="S3.p1.m21" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="u^{i}_{t=0:T-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u^{i}_{t=0:T-1}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the control sequence at the reverse diffusion step <math id="S3.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in[N,N-1,\dots,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in[N,N-1,\dots,1]</annotation></semantics></math>. The probability <math id="S3.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{i}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{i}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> is the outcome of iteratively adding Gaussian noise to the target distribution <math id="S3.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{0}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{0}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math>. At <math id="S3.p1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=N</annotation></semantics></math>, the control sequence is sampled from a multi-variate normal distribution <math id="S3.p1.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}(0,I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}(0,I)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.p1.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the <math id="S3.p1.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{Tm\times Tm}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{Tm\times Tm}</annotation></semantics></math> identity matrix. In (<a href="#S3.E6" title="Equation 6 ‣ III Background on diffusion for Trajectory Optimization ‣ PAD-TRO: Projection-Augmented Diffusion for Direct Trajectory Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>), <math id="S3.p1.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\alpha}_{i}=\prod_{k=1}^{i}\alpha_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>i</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\alpha}_{i}=\prod_{k=1}^{i}\alpha_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, where each <math id="S3.p1.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as <math id="S3.p1.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-\beta_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-\beta_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.p1.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> linearly and uniformly increases from an initial <math id="S3.p1.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{0}\ll 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>≪</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{0}\ll 1</annotation></semantics></math> to a final <math id="S3.p1.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{N}\ll 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>≪</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{N}\ll 1</annotation></semantics></math>. The score function <math id="S3.p1.m35" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{u}\log p_{i}(u^{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>u</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{u}\log p_{i}(u^{i})</annotation></semantics></math> at each reverse diffusion step <math id="S3.p1.m36" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> is computed via Monte Carlo estimation via parallel dynamics roll-outs: first, at each reverse diffusion step <math id="S3.p1.m37" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, a batch of <math id="S3.p1.m38" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> samples of control sequences is drawn as</p>
<table id="S3.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U^{i}\sim\mathcal{N}\left(\frac{u^{i}}{\sqrt{\bar{\alpha}_{i-1}}},\left(\frac{1}{\bar{\alpha}_{i-1}}-1\right)I\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>U</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><msqrt><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></msqrt></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mfrac><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U^{i}\sim\mathcal{N}\left(\frac{u^{i}}{\sqrt{\bar{\alpha}_{i-1}}},\left(\frac{1}{\bar{\alpha}_{i-1}}-1\right)I\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p1.6" class="ltx_p">where the batch <math id="S3.p1.m39" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U^{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{N_{s}\times Tm}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>U</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U^{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{N_{s}\times Tm}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then, a weighted average sample is computed as</p>
<table id="S3.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{u}^{i}=\frac{\sum_{u\in U^{i}}up_{J}(u)p_{g}(u)}{\sum_{u\in U^{i}}p_{J}(u)p_{g}(u)}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>U</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>J</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>U</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>J</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{u}^{i}=\frac{\sum_{u\in U^{i}}up_{J}(u)p_{g}(u)}{\sum_{u\in U^{i}}p_{J}(u)p_{g}(u)}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p1.7" class="ltx_p">To evaluate <math id="S3.p1.m40" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{J}(u)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>J</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{J}(u)</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="S3.p1.m41" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\propto" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∝</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\propto</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="S3.p1.m42" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="e^{-J(\cdot)/\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>/</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e^{-J(\cdot)/\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math>, the control sequence <math id="S3.p1.m43" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> is applied to the system to obtain a sequence of dynamically feasible states, which is then evaluated by the cost function <math id="S3.p1.m44" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="J(x_{1:T},u_{0:T-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J(x_{1:T},u_{0:T-1})</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, the dynamic feasibility <math id="S3.p1.m45" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{d}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{d}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> is implicitly enforced. For safety constraints <math id="S3.p1.m46" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{g}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{g}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math>, if the drawn samples lead to collisions, then they are excluded from the weighted average sample in (<a href="#S3.E8" title="Equation 8 ‣ III Background on diffusion for Trajectory Optimization ‣ PAD-TRO: Projection-Augmented Diffusion for Direct Trajectory Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>).
Next, the score function is approximated as follows using the weighted average sample above:</p>
<table id="S3.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{u^{i}}\log p_{i}(u^{i})\approx-\frac{u^{i}-\sqrt{\bar{\alpha}_{i}}\,\bar{u}^{i}}{1-\bar{\alpha}_{i}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mrow><msqrt><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></msqrt><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{u^{i}}\log p_{i}(u^{i})\approx-\frac{u^{i}-\sqrt{\bar{\alpha}_{i}}\,\bar{u}^{i}}{1-\bar{\alpha}_{i}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p1.8" class="ltx_p">At the end of the reverse diffusion process (<a href="#S3.E6" title="Equation 6 ‣ III Background on diffusion for Trajectory Optimization ‣ PAD-TRO: Projection-Augmented Diffusion for Direct Trajectory Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>), the final denoised control sequence <math id="S3.p1.m47" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>u</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u^{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is executed to forward propagate the system <math id="S3.p1.m48" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{t+1}=f(x_{t},u_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{t+1}=f(x_{t},u_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="S3.p1.m49" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> to obtain a dynamically feasible state trajectory <math id="S3.p1.m50" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="x_{t=0:T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{t=0:T}</annotation></semantics></math>. Overall, MBD <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite> applies model-based diffusion to sample from (<a href="#S2.E2" title="Equation 2 ‣ II PROBLEM FORMULATION ‣ PAD-TRO: Projection-Augmented Diffusion for Direct Trajectory Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>), where only the control <math id="S3.p1.m51" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="u_{0:T-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{0:T-1}</annotation></semantics></math> are the decision variables. This framework is analogous to single shooting in classic gradient-based trajectory optimization.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">IV </span><span id="S4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">CONSTRAINED DIFFUSION FOR DIRECT TRAJECTORY OPTIMIZATION</span></h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">One significant drawback of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite> is that in waypoint navigation scenarios in a cluttered environment, the optimized trajectory cannot reach the target exactly, leading to sub-optimal solutions. MBD <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite> is unable to achieve close convergence since the terminal constraint <math id="S4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{T}\in\mathcal{X}_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{T}\in\mathcal{X}_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> cannot be enforced due to its single-shooting-like nature.
In addition, random trajectory rollouts in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite> lead to many collisions in a cluttered environment, and thus the weighted sample average in the score estimation (<a href="#S3.E9" title="Equation 9 ‣ III Background on diffusion for Trajectory Optimization ‣ PAD-TRO: Projection-Augmented Diffusion for Direct Trajectory Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>) consists of very sparse samples. Consequently, the score function estimated using these random rollouts constitutes a weak “guidance signal”. In contrast, in Section <a href="#S4.SS2" title="IV-B Dynamic feasibility via gradient-free projection ‣ IV CONSTRAINED DIFFUSION FOR DIRECT TRAJECTORY OPTIMIZATION ‣ PAD-TRO: Projection-Augmented Diffusion for Direct Trajectory Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">IV-B</span></a> we propose a constrained diffusion process for direct trajectory optimization by directly sampling the sequence of <span id="S4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">predicted states</span> <math id="S4.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\tilde{x}^{i}_{t=1:T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{x}^{i}_{t=1:T}</annotation></semantics></math>. Due to the fact that diffusion models, either data-drive or model-based, cannot automatically produce dynamically feasible states, we draw inspiration from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite> to enforce the dynamic feasibility of the predicted sequence of states via a sequential projection scheme. In contrast to the projection scheme in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>, which requires solving a convex optimization problem to project each predicted state <math id="S4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{x}_{t+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{x}_{t+1}</annotation></semantics></math> onto the reachable set of <math id="S4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{x}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{x}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, we adopt a sampling-based approach to keep the projection scheme gradient-free. We introduce our diffusion-based trajectory optimization framework in Section <a href="#S4.SS1" title="IV-A Diffusion for direct sampling of state trajectory ‣ IV CONSTRAINED DIFFUSION FOR DIRECT TRAJECTORY OPTIMIZATION ‣ PAD-TRO: Projection-Augmented Diffusion for Direct Trajectory Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">IV-A</span></a> by considering optimality <math id="S4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{J}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>J</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{J}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> and safety <math id="S4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{g}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{g}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, we discuss the details of our projection scheme in Section. <a href="#S4.SS2" title="IV-B Dynamic feasibility via gradient-free projection ‣ IV CONSTRAINED DIFFUSION FOR DIRECT TRAJECTORY OPTIMIZATION ‣ PAD-TRO: Projection-Augmented Diffusion for Direct Trajectory Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">IV-B</span></a> to augment our approach and satisfy dynamic feasibility <math id="S4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{d}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{d}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS1.6" class="ltx_text">IV-A</span> </span><span id="S4.SS1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Diffusion for direct sampling of state trajectory</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To gradually generate samples that follow the target distribution <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{0}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{0}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> (<a href="#S2.E2" title="Equation 2 ‣ II PROBLEM FORMULATION ‣ PAD-TRO: Projection-Augmented Diffusion for Direct Trajectory Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>), we propose the following approach built upon <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite> by introducing a novel gradient-free projection scheme and a bi-level noise schedule. In <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>, the noise schedule, i.e., the standard deviation of the Gaussian (<a href="#S4.E12" title="Equation 12 ‣ IV-A Diffusion for direct sampling of state trajectory ‣ IV CONSTRAINED DIFFUSION FOR DIRECT TRAJECTORY OPTIMIZATION ‣ PAD-TRO: Projection-Augmented Diffusion for Direct Trajectory Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>), <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{i}:=\sqrt{\frac{1-\bar{\alpha}_{i-1}}{\bar{\alpha}_{i-1}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><msqrt><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mfrac></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{i}:=\sqrt{\frac{1-\bar{\alpha}_{i-1}}{\bar{\alpha}_{i-1}}}</annotation></semantics></math> decays only along the diffusion horizon. Inspired by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>, we propose a bi-level noise schedule <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{i,t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{i,t}</annotation></semantics></math>, where the noise not only varies along the diffusion horizon <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=N,N-1,\dots,1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=N,N-1,\dots,1</annotation></semantics></math>, but also varies along the trajectory prediction horizon, <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=1,\dots,T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=1,\dots,T</annotation></semantics></math>. In practice, we find that this allows for a better trade-off between exploration and reaching optimality in the reverse diffusion process.
We define the novel noise schedule as follows:</p>
<table id="S4.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{i,t}=\sqrt{\frac{1-\bar{\alpha}_{i-1}}{\bar{\alpha}_{i-1}}}\cdot\delta^{t},i\in[N,\dots,1],t\in[1,\dots,T]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msqrt><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mfrac></msqrt><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mi>δ</mi><mi>t</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{i,t}=\sqrt{\frac{1-\bar{\alpha}_{i-1}}{\bar{\alpha}_{i-1}}}\cdot\delta^{t},i\in[N,\dots,1],t\in[1,\dots,T].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta\in(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\in(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math> controls the noise along the trajectory prediction horizon. Intuitively, the states at time steps later along the trajectory prediction horizon <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=1,\dots T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=1,\dots T</annotation></semantics></math> receive lower noise, allowing for effective projection of predicted states onto the reachable sets of earlier states, which we discuss in detail in Section <a href="#S4.SS2" title="IV-B Dynamic feasibility via gradient-free projection ‣ IV CONSTRAINED DIFFUSION FOR DIRECT TRAJECTORY OPTIMIZATION ‣ PAD-TRO: Projection-Augmented Diffusion for Direct Trajectory Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">IV-B</span></a>. In addition, <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\alpha_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\alpha_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> are defined as in Section <a href="#S3" title="III Background on diffusion for Trajectory Optimization ‣ PAD-TRO: Projection-Augmented Diffusion for Direct Trajectory Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">III</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">While MBD <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite> applies forward rollouts via the control inputs to discard samples that result in collisions per the constraints <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{g}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{g}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math>, we apply the following exponential collision avoidance cost:</p>
<table id="S4.E11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(x)=\sum_{i=1}^{N_{\mathrm{obs}}}\exp\!\left(-\kappa\,\big(\|\,o(x)-o_{obs,i}\,\|_{2}^{2}-r_{i}^{2}\big)\right),\kappa&gt;0," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>obs</mi></msub></munderover><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.370em;" width="1.370em"><mi>exp</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo lspace="0.170em">⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(x)=\sum_{i=1}^{N_{\mathrm{obs}}}\exp\!\left(-\kappa\,\big(\|\,o(x)-o_{obs,i}\,\|_{2}^{2}-r_{i}^{2}\big)\right),\kappa&gt;0,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where we denote <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o(x)</annotation></semantics></math> as the position of the trajectory and <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o_{obs,i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>o</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o_{obs,i}</annotation></semantics></math> is the position of the <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i^{th}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i^{th}</annotation></semantics></math> obstacle. We also denote <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> as the radius of the <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i^{th}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i^{th}</annotation></semantics></math> obstacle. We drop the time indices here for simplicity. When <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{g}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{g}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> is combined with optimality <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{J}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>J</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{J}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math>, the estimated score function is weighted by both functions. In the reverse diffusion process, this has the effect of pushing the denoised trajectories toward a probability distribution that satisfies both inequality constraints, i.e., staying away from obstacles, and lowering the trajectory cost simultaneously. Furthermore, rather than diffusing the inputs to the system as in (<a href="#S3.E6" title="Equation 6 ‣ III Background on diffusion for Trajectory Optimization ‣ PAD-TRO: Projection-Augmented Diffusion for Direct Trajectory Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>), we diffuse the states. This allows us to enforce the terminal constraint and to reduce the number of trajectories that collide with the obstacles.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To be formal, we initialize the state trajectory <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{x}^{N}\in\mathbb{R}^{T\times n}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>N</mi></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{x}^{N}\in\mathbb{R}^{T\times n}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,I)</annotation></semantics></math>. At each reverse diffusion step <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, a batch of <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> samples of state trajectories is drawn from the Gaussian distribution (line 3, Algorithm <a href="#algorithm1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ IV-A Diffusion for direct sampling of state trajectory ‣ IV CONSTRAINED DIFFUSION FOR DIRECT TRAJECTORY OPTIMIZATION ‣ PAD-TRO: Projection-Augmented Diffusion for Direct Trajectory Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>)</p>
<table id="S4.E12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{X}^{i}\sim\mathcal{N}\left(\frac{\tilde{x}^{i}}{\sqrt{\bar{\alpha}_{i-1}}},\sigma^{2}_{i,t}I\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msup><msqrt><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></msqrt></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{X}^{i}\sim\mathcal{N}\left(\frac{\tilde{x}^{i}}{\sqrt{\bar{\alpha}_{i-1}}},\sigma^{2}_{i,t}I\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where the dimension of <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{X}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{X}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{N_{s}\times T\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{N_{s}\times T\times n}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, at the reverse diffusion step <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, a weighted sample mean (line 5, Algorithm <a href="#algorithm1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ IV-A Diffusion for direct sampling of state trajectory ‣ IV CONSTRAINED DIFFUSION FOR DIRECT TRAJECTORY OPTIMIZATION ‣ PAD-TRO: Projection-Augmented Diffusion for Direct Trajectory Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>)</p>
<table id="S4.E13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{x}^{i}=\frac{\sum_{x\in\tilde{X}^{i}}xp_{J}(x)p_{g}(x)}{\sum_{x\in\tilde{X}^{i}}p_{J}(x)p_{g}(x)}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>J</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>J</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{x}^{i}=\frac{\sum_{x\in\tilde{X}^{i}}xp_{J}(x)p_{g}(x)}{\sum_{x\in\tilde{X}^{i}}p_{J}(x)p_{g}(x)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.3" class="ltx_p">is used to approximate the score function <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{\tilde{x}^{i}}\log p_{i}(\tilde{x}^{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msup></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{\tilde{x}^{i}}\log p_{i}(\tilde{x}^{i})</annotation></semantics></math> (line 6, Algorithm <a href="#algorithm1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ IV-A Diffusion for direct sampling of state trajectory ‣ IV CONSTRAINED DIFFUSION FOR DIRECT TRAJECTORY OPTIMIZATION ‣ PAD-TRO: Projection-Augmented Diffusion for Direct Trajectory Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>)</p>
<table id="S4.E14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{\tilde{x}^{i}}\log p_{i}(\tilde{x}^{i})\approx-\frac{\tilde{x}^{i}-\sqrt{\bar{\alpha}_{i}}\,\bar{x}^{i}}{1-\bar{\alpha}_{i}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msup></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mrow><msqrt><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></msqrt><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{\tilde{x}^{i}}\log p_{i}(\tilde{x}^{i})\approx-\frac{\tilde{x}^{i}-\sqrt{\bar{\alpha}_{i}}\,\bar{x}^{i}}{1-\bar{\alpha}_{i}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.4" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> results from iteratively adding Gaussian noise to <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>. The reverse diffusion process is given by (line 7, Algorithm <a href="#algorithm1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ IV-A Diffusion for direct sampling of state trajectory ‣ IV CONSTRAINED DIFFUSION FOR DIRECT TRAJECTORY OPTIMIZATION ‣ PAD-TRO: Projection-Augmented Diffusion for Direct Trajectory Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>)</p>
<table id="S4.E15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{x}^{i-1}=\frac{1}{\sqrt{\alpha_{i}}}(\tilde{x}^{i}+(1-\bar{\alpha}_{i})\nabla_{\tilde{x}^{i}}\log p_{i}(\tilde{x}^{i}))." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msqrt><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msqrt></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mo>∇</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msup></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{x}^{i-1}=\frac{1}{\sqrt{\alpha_{i}}}(\tilde{x}^{i}+(1-\bar{\alpha}_{i})\nabla_{\tilde{x}^{i}}\log p_{i}(\tilde{x}^{i})).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(15)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.5" class="ltx_p">To address the terminal set constraint <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{T}\in\mathcal{X}_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{T}\in\mathcal{X}_{T}</annotation></semantics></math>, we fix the end state by enforcing the boundary condition at <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{x}^{i}_{T}:=x_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>:=</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{x}^{i}_{T}:=x_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> while setting <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{i,T}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{i,T}=0</annotation></semantics></math>. Intuitively, at each reverse diffusion step <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, our direct sampling approach generates a sequence of states <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m14" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\tilde{x}^{i}_{t=1:T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{x}^{i}_{t=1:T}</annotation></semantics></math>.
In <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>, the authors prove that when <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> is small enough, the distribution of <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J(X)</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\sim p_{0}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\sim p_{0}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> approaches the optimal cost <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>J</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> under mild conditions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We now show that the score function (<a href="#S4.E14" title="Equation 14 ‣ IV-A Diffusion for direct sampling of state trajectory ‣ IV CONSTRAINED DIFFUSION FOR DIRECT TRAJECTORY OPTIMIZATION ‣ PAD-TRO: Projection-Augmented Diffusion for Direct Trajectory Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>) can indeed be expressed as a linear combination of the predicted state sequence <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{x}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{x}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and a weighted sample mean <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{x}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{x}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math> in the following proposition.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmproposition1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmproposition1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 1</span></span><span id="Thmproposition1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmproposition1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmproposition1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmproposition1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The score function (<a href="#S4.E14" title="Equation 14 ‣ IV-A Diffusion for direct sampling of state trajectory ‣ IV CONSTRAINED DIFFUSION FOR DIRECT TRAJECTORY OPTIMIZATION ‣ PAD-TRO: Projection-Augmented Diffusion for Direct Trajectory Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>) can be approximated as <math id="Thmproposition1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{\tilde{x}^{i}}\log p_{i}(\tilde{x}^{i})\approx-\frac{-\tilde{x}^{i}-\sqrt{\bar{\alpha}_{i}}\bar{x}^{i}}{1-\bar{\alpha}_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msup></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msqrt><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{\tilde{x}^{i}}\log p_{i}(\tilde{x}^{i})\approx-\frac{-\tilde{x}^{i}-\sqrt{\bar{\alpha}_{i}}\bar{x}^{i}}{1-\bar{\alpha}_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="Thmproposition1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{x}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{x}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is a weighted sample mean (<a href="#S4.E13" title="Equation 13 ‣ IV-A Diffusion for direct sampling of state trajectory ‣ IV CONSTRAINED DIFFUSION FOR DIRECT TRAJECTORY OPTIMIZATION ‣ PAD-TRO: Projection-Augmented Diffusion for Direct Trajectory Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>).</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.8" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S4.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">The proof is partially adapted from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>. We first define the following “forward process”, which iteratively adds Gaussian noise to a target distribution <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{0}(\cdot)\propto p_{J}(\cdot)p_{g}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∝</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>J</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{0}(\cdot)\propto p_{J}(\cdot)p_{g}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math>:</span></p>
<table id="S4.E16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E16.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="p_{i|0}(\cdot|\tilde{x}^{0})\sim\mathcal{N}\left(\sqrt{\bar{\alpha}_{i}}\tilde{x}^{0},(1-\bar{\alpha}_{i})I)\right),\bar{\alpha}_{i}=\prod_{k=1}^{i}\alpha_{k}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><msqrt><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></msqrt><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>i</mi></munderover><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{i|0}(\cdot|\tilde{x}^{0})\sim\mathcal{N}\left(\sqrt{\bar{\alpha}_{i}}\tilde{x}^{0},(1-\bar{\alpha}_{i})I)\right),\bar{\alpha}_{i}=\prod_{k=1}^{i}\alpha_{k}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(16)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p5.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p5.2.1" class="ltx_text">We may also define the forward process at <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> conditioned on <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i-1</annotation></semantics></math>, given as:</span></p>
<table id="S4.E17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E17.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="p_{i|i-1}(\cdot|\tilde{x}^{i-1})\sim\mathcal{N}\left(\sqrt{\alpha_{i}}\tilde{x}^{i-1},(1-\alpha_{i})I)\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><msqrt><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msqrt><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{i|i-1}(\cdot|\tilde{x}^{i-1})\sim\mathcal{N}\left(\sqrt{\alpha_{i}}\tilde{x}^{i-1},(1-\alpha_{i})I)\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(17)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p5.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p5.3.1" class="ltx_text">Our reverse diffusion process (<a href="#S4.E15" title="Equation 15 ‣ IV-A Diffusion for direct sampling of state trajectory ‣ IV CONSTRAINED DIFFUSION FOR DIRECT TRAJECTORY OPTIMIZATION ‣ PAD-TRO: Projection-Augmented Diffusion for Direct Trajectory Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a>) <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{i-1|i}(\tilde{x}^{i-1}|\tilde{x}^{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{i-1|i}(\tilde{x}^{i-1}|\tilde{x}^{i})</annotation></semantics></math> stems from the reverse of the forward process <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{i|i-1}(\tilde{x}^{i}|\tilde{x}^{i-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{i|i-1}(\tilde{x}^{i}|\tilde{x}^{i-1})</annotation></semantics></math>. From Bayes’ rule, we have:</span></p>
<table id="S4.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E18"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\nabla_{x^{i}}\log p_{i}(x^{i})=\frac{\nabla_{\tilde{x}^{i}}\int p_{i|0}\!\left(\tilde{x}^{i}\mid\tilde{x}^{0}\right)p_{0}(\tilde{x}^{(0)})\,d\tilde{x}^{(0)}}{\int p_{i|0}\!\left(\tilde{x}^{i}\mid\tilde{x}^{0}\right)p_{0}(\tilde{x}^{0})\,d\tilde{x}^{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mo>∇</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msup></msub><mo>∫</mo><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo>(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msup><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msup><mo>)</mo><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">)</mo><mi>d</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msup><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\nabla_{x^{i}}\log p_{i}(x^{i})=\frac{\nabla_{\tilde{x}^{i}}\int p_{i|0}\!\left(\tilde{x}^{i}\mid\tilde{x}^{0}\right)p_{0}(\tilde{x}^{(0)})\,d\tilde{x}^{(0)}}{\int p_{i|0}\!\left(\tilde{x}^{i}\mid\tilde{x}^{0}\right)p_{0}(\tilde{x}^{0})\,d\tilde{x}^{0}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(18)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.E19"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{\int\dfrac{-\tilde{x}^{i}-\sqrt{\bar{\alpha}_{i}}\,\tilde{x}^{0}}{1-\bar{\alpha}_{i}}\,p_{i|0}\!\left(\tilde{x}^{i}\mid\tilde{x}^{0}\right)p_{0}(\tilde{x}^{0})\,d\tilde{x}^{0}}{\int p_{i|0}\!\left(\tilde{x}^{i}\mid\tilde{x}^{0}\right)p_{0}(\tilde{x}^{0})\,d\tilde{x}^{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msqrt><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></msqrt><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msup><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msup><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{\int\dfrac{-\tilde{x}^{i}-\sqrt{\bar{\alpha}_{i}}\,\tilde{x}^{0}}{1-\bar{\alpha}_{i}}\,p_{i|0}\!\left(\tilde{x}^{i}\mid\tilde{x}^{0}\right)p_{0}(\tilde{x}^{0})\,d\tilde{x}^{0}}{\int p_{i|0}\!\left(\tilde{x}^{i}\mid\tilde{x}^{0}\right)p_{0}(\tilde{x}^{0})\,d\tilde{x}^{0}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(19)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.E20"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=-\frac{\tilde{x}^{i}}{1-\bar{\alpha}_{i}}+\frac{\sqrt{\bar{\alpha}_{i}}}{1-\bar{\alpha}_{i}}\,\frac{\int\tilde{x}^{0}p_{i|0}\!\left(\tilde{x}^{i}\mid\tilde{x}^{0}\right)p_{0}(\tilde{x}^{0})\,d\tilde{x}^{0}}{\int p_{i|0}\!\left(\tilde{x}^{i}\mid\tilde{x}^{0}\right)p_{0}(\tilde{x}^{0})\,d\tilde{x}^{0}}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msup><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msqrt><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msup><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msup><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=-\frac{\tilde{x}^{i}}{1-\bar{\alpha}_{i}}+\frac{\sqrt{\bar{\alpha}_{i}}}{1-\bar{\alpha}_{i}}\,\frac{\int\tilde{x}^{0}p_{i|0}\!\left(\tilde{x}^{i}\mid\tilde{x}^{0}\right)p_{0}(\tilde{x}^{0})\,d\tilde{x}^{0}}{\int p_{i|0}\!\left(\tilde{x}^{i}\mid\tilde{x}^{0}\right)p_{0}(\tilde{x}^{0})\,d\tilde{x}^{0}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(20)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p5.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p5.4.1" class="ltx_text">where the forward Gaussian (<a href="#S4.E16" title="Equation 16 ‣ Proof. ‣ IV-A Diffusion for direct sampling of state trajectory ‣ IV CONSTRAINED DIFFUSION FOR DIRECT TRAJECTORY OPTIMIZATION ‣ PAD-TRO: Projection-Augmented Diffusion for Direct Trajectory Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">16</span></a>) is used to evaluate the gradient. Then, by completing the squares we can show that:</span></p>
<table id="S4.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E21"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle p_{i|0}(\tilde{x}^{i}\mid\tilde{x}^{0})\;\propto\;\exp\!\left(-\tfrac{1}{2}\frac{\bigl(\tilde{x}^{0}-\tfrac{\tilde{x}^{i}}{\sqrt{\bar{\alpha}_{i}}}\bigr)^{\!\top}\bigl(\tilde{x}^{0}-\tfrac{\tilde{x}^{i}}{\sqrt{\bar{\alpha}_{i}}}\bigr)}{\tfrac{1-\bar{\alpha}_{i}}{\bar{\alpha}_{i}}}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msup><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msup><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">∝</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.370em;" width="1.370em"><mi>exp</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mfrac><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msup><msqrt><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></msqrt></mfrac></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mfrac><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msup><msqrt><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></msqrt></mfrac></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></mfrac></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle p_{i|0}(\tilde{x}^{i}\mid\tilde{x}^{0})\;\propto\;\exp\!\left(-\tfrac{1}{2}\frac{\bigl(\tilde{x}^{0}-\tfrac{\tilde{x}^{i}}{\sqrt{\bar{\alpha}_{i}}}\bigr)^{\!\top}\bigl(\tilde{x}^{0}-\tfrac{\tilde{x}^{i}}{\sqrt{\bar{\alpha}_{i}}}\bigr)}{\tfrac{1-\bar{\alpha}_{i}}{\bar{\alpha}_{i}}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(21)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.E22"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\;\propto\;\mathcal{N}\!\left(\tfrac{\tilde{x}^{i}}{\sqrt{\bar{\alpha}_{i}}},\;(\tfrac{1}{\bar{\alpha}_{i}}-1)I\right)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">∝</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.798em;" width="0.798em"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msup><msqrt><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></msqrt></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></mfrac><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\;\propto\;\mathcal{N}\!\left(\tfrac{\tilde{x}^{i}}{\sqrt{\bar{\alpha}_{i}}},\;(\tfrac{1}{\bar{\alpha}_{i}}-1)I\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(22)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p5.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p5.5.1" class="ltx_text">Note that (<a href="#S4.E22" title="Equation 22 ‣ Proof. ‣ IV-A Diffusion for direct sampling of state trajectory ‣ IV CONSTRAINED DIFFUSION FOR DIRECT TRAJECTORY OPTIMIZATION ‣ PAD-TRO: Projection-Augmented Diffusion for Direct Trajectory Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">22</span></a>) is equivalent to (<a href="#S4.E12" title="Equation 12 ‣ IV-A Diffusion for direct sampling of state trajectory ‣ IV CONSTRAINED DIFFUSION FOR DIRECT TRAJECTORY OPTIMIZATION ‣ PAD-TRO: Projection-Augmented Diffusion for Direct Trajectory Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>), which is our batch sampling step. We substitute (<a href="#S4.E22" title="Equation 22 ‣ Proof. ‣ IV-A Diffusion for direct sampling of state trajectory ‣ IV CONSTRAINED DIFFUSION FOR DIRECT TRAJECTORY OPTIMIZATION ‣ PAD-TRO: Projection-Augmented Diffusion for Direct Trajectory Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">22</span></a>) into (<a href="#S4.E20" title="Equation 20 ‣ Proof. ‣ IV-A Diffusion for direct sampling of state trajectory ‣ IV CONSTRAINED DIFFUSION FOR DIRECT TRAJECTORY OPTIMIZATION ‣ PAD-TRO: Projection-Augmented Diffusion for Direct Trajectory Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">20</span></a>)
and replace the continuous integral term in (<a href="#S4.E20" title="Equation 20 ‣ Proof. ‣ IV-A Diffusion for direct sampling of state trajectory ‣ IV CONSTRAINED DIFFUSION FOR DIRECT TRAJECTORY OPTIMIZATION ‣ PAD-TRO: Projection-Augmented Diffusion for Direct Trajectory Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">20</span></a>) with Monte Carlo estimation. We treat each <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{x}^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{x}^{0}</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S4.E20" title="Equation 20 ‣ Proof. ‣ IV-A Diffusion for direct sampling of state trajectory ‣ IV CONSTRAINED DIFFUSION FOR DIRECT TRAJECTORY OPTIMIZATION ‣ PAD-TRO: Projection-Augmented Diffusion for Direct Trajectory Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">20</span></a>) as a sample drawn from (<a href="#S4.E22" title="Equation 22 ‣ Proof. ‣ IV-A Diffusion for direct sampling of state trajectory ‣ IV CONSTRAINED DIFFUSION FOR DIRECT TRAJECTORY OPTIMIZATION ‣ PAD-TRO: Projection-Augmented Diffusion for Direct Trajectory Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">22</span></a>) since the integration variable is <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{x}^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{x}^{0}</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, we obtain:</span></p>
<table id="S4.E23" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{x^{i}}\log p_{i}(x^{i})=-\frac{\tilde{x}^{i}}{1-\bar{\alpha}_{i}}+\frac{\sqrt{\bar{\alpha}_{i}}}{1-\bar{\alpha}_{i}}\,\frac{\sum_{x\in\tilde{X}_{i}}p_{J}(x)p_{g}(x)}{\sum_{x\in\tilde{X}_{i}}p_{J}(x)p_{g}(x)}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msup><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><msqrt><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>J</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>J</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{x^{i}}\log p_{i}(x^{i})=-\frac{\tilde{x}^{i}}{1-\bar{\alpha}_{i}}+\frac{\sqrt{\bar{\alpha}_{i}}}{1-\bar{\alpha}_{i}}\,\frac{\sum_{x\in\tilde{X}_{i}}p_{J}(x)p_{g}(x)}{\sum_{x\in\tilde{X}_{i}}p_{J}(x)p_{g}(x)},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(23)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p5.6" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p5.6.1" class="ltx_text">By defining</span></p>
<table id="S4.E24" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{x}_{i}=\frac{\sum_{x\in\tilde{X}_{i}}xp_{J}(x)p_{g}(x)}{\sum_{x\in\tilde{X}_{i}}p_{J}(x)p_{g}(x)}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>J</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>J</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{x}_{i}=\frac{\sum_{x\in\tilde{X}_{i}}xp_{J}(x)p_{g}(x)}{\sum_{x\in\tilde{X}_{i}}p_{J}(x)p_{g}(x)},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(24)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p5.7" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p5.7.1" class="ltx_text">we are able to establish the result that</span></p>
<table id="S4.E25" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{\tilde{x}^{i}}\log p_{i}(\tilde{x}^{i})\approx-\frac{\tilde{x}^{i}-\sqrt{\bar{\alpha}_{i}}\bar{x}^{i}}{1-\bar{\alpha}_{i}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msup></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mrow><msqrt><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{\tilde{x}^{i}}\log p_{i}(\tilde{x}^{i})\approx-\frac{\tilde{x}^{i}-\sqrt{\bar{\alpha}_{i}}\bar{x}^{i}}{1-\bar{\alpha}_{i}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(25)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p5.8" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p5.8.1" class="ltx_text">∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">It is noted that dynamic feasibility <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{d}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{d}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> is not explicitly considered in (<a href="#S4.E13" title="Equation 13 ‣ IV-A Diffusion for direct sampling of state trajectory ‣ IV CONSTRAINED DIFFUSION FOR DIRECT TRAJECTORY OPTIMIZATION ‣ PAD-TRO: Projection-Augmented Diffusion for Direct Trajectory Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>) since <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{d}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{d}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> is enforced by directly applying projection to <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{X}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{X}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S4.E12" title="Equation 12 ‣ IV-A Diffusion for direct sampling of state trajectory ‣ IV CONSTRAINED DIFFUSION FOR DIRECT TRAJECTORY OPTIMIZATION ‣ PAD-TRO: Projection-Augmented Diffusion for Direct Trajectory Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>). We discuss our novel approach for enforcing dynamic feasibility via projection in the following section.</p>
</div>
<figure id="algorithm1" class="ltx_float ltx_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="algorithm1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span>Projection Augmented Model-Based Diffusion for Direct Trajectory Optimization</figcaption>
<div id="algorithm1.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_lst_numbers_left ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="algorithm1.4.1" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Input :</span> <math id="algorithm1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{x}^{N}\in\mathbb{R}^{(T-1)\times n}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>N</mi></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{x}^{N}\in\mathbb{R}^{(T-1)\times n}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,I)</annotation></semantics></math> 
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.2" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">1</span></span>
            Initialize noise schedule <math id="algorithm1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{i,t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{i,t}</annotation></semantics></math> 
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.3" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">2</span></span>
            <span id="algorithm1.4.3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> <em id="algorithm1.4.3.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic"><math id="algorithm1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\leftarrow N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\leftarrow N</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="algorithm1.4.3.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_upright">to</span> <math id="algorithm1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math></em> <span id="algorithm1.4.3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.4" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">3</span></span>
             <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> 
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">4</span></span>
            <span id="algorithm1.4.4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Sample a batch</span> 
<table id="S4.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{X}^{(i)}\sim\mathcal{N}\Bigl(\tfrac{\tilde{x}^{i}}{\sqrt{\bar{\alpha}_{i-1}}},\,\sigma_{i,t}^{2}I\Bigr)\;" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msup><msqrt><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></msqrt></mfrac></mstyle><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{X}^{(i)}\sim\mathcal{N}\Bigl(\tfrac{\tilde{x}^{i}}{\sqrt{\bar{\alpha}_{i-1}}},\,\sigma_{i,t}^{2}I\Bigr)\;</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.5" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">5</span></span>
             <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> 
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">6</span></span>
            <span id="algorithm1.4.5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Batch projection</span>: 
<table id="S4.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{X}^{(i)}\leftarrow\texttt{project}\bigl(\tilde{X}^{(i)}\bigr)\;" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">project</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{X}^{(i)}\leftarrow\texttt{project}\bigl(\tilde{X}^{(i)}\bigr)\;</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.6" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">7</span></span>
             <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> 
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">8</span></span>
            <span id="algorithm1.4.6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Compute weighted mean</span>:

<table id="S4.Ex3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex3X"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex3X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\bar{x}^{(i)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\bar{x}^{(i)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex3X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{\sum_{x\in\tilde{X}^{(i)}}x\,p_{J}(x)p_{g}(x)}{\sum_{x\in\tilde{X}^{(i)}}p_{J}(x)p_{g}(x)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>J</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>J</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{\sum_{x\in\tilde{X}^{(i)}}x\,p_{J}(x)p_{g}(x)}{\sum_{x\in\tilde{X}^{(i)}}p_{J}(x)p_{g}(x)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.7" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">9</span></span>
             <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> 
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">10</span></span>
            <span id="algorithm1.4.7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Estimate score</span>:

<table id="S4.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{\tilde{x}^{i}}\log p_{i}(\tilde{x}^{i})\approx-\frac{\tilde{x}^{i}-\sqrt{\bar{\alpha}_{i}}\,\bar{x}^{i}}{1-\bar{\alpha}_{i}}\;" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msup></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mrow><msqrt><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></msqrt><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{\tilde{x}^{i}}\log p_{i}(\tilde{x}^{i})\approx-\frac{\tilde{x}^{i}-\sqrt{\bar{\alpha}_{i}}\,\bar{x}^{i}}{1-\bar{\alpha}_{i}}\;</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.8" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.8.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">11</span></span>
             <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> 
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">12</span></span>
            <span id="algorithm1.4.8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Update sample</span>:

<table id="S4.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{x}^{i-1}=\frac{1}{\sqrt{\alpha_{i}}}\Bigl(\tilde{x}^{i}+(1-\bar{\alpha}_{i})\,\nabla_{\tilde{x}^{i}}\log p_{i}(\tilde{x}^{i})\Bigr)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msqrt><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msqrt></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.337em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mo>∇</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msup></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{x}^{i-1}=\frac{1}{\sqrt{\alpha_{i}}}\Bigl(\tilde{x}^{i}+(1-\bar{\alpha}_{i})\,\nabla_{\tilde{x}^{i}}\log p_{i}(\tilde{x}^{i})\Bigr)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.9" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.9.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">13</span></span>
             <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> 
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">14</span></span>
            <span id="algorithm1.4.9.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Project single sample</span>:

<table id="S4.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{x}^{i-1}\leftarrow\texttt{project}\bigl(\tilde{x}^{i-1}\bigr)\;" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">project</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{x}^{i-1}\leftarrow\texttt{project}\bigl(\tilde{x}^{i-1}\bigr)\;</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.10" class="ltx_listingline">
</div>
</div>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS2.6" class="ltx_text">IV-B</span> </span><span id="S4.SS2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Dynamic feasibility via gradient-free projection</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">While we have introduced our direct trajectory sampling approach in Section <a href="#S4.SS1" title="IV-A Diffusion for direct sampling of state trajectory ‣ IV CONSTRAINED DIFFUSION FOR DIRECT TRAJECTORY OPTIMIZATION ‣ PAD-TRO: Projection-Augmented Diffusion for Direct Trajectory Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">IV-A</span></a> that gradually generates samples from the target distribution <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{0}(\cdot)\sim p_{J}(\cdot)p_{g}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>J</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{0}(\cdot)\sim p_{J}(\cdot)p_{g}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math>, we have not yet accounted for dynamic feasibility <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{d}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{d}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore, we augment our approach in Section <a href="#S4.SS1" title="IV-A Diffusion for direct sampling of state trajectory ‣ IV CONSTRAINED DIFFUSION FOR DIRECT TRAJECTORY OPTIMIZATION ‣ PAD-TRO: Projection-Augmented Diffusion for Direct Trajectory Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">IV-A</span></a> by introducing a gradient-free projection to enforce dynamic feasibility recursively to complete our algorithm. Note that the predicted state sequence at reverse diffusion step <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e., <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\tilde{x}^{i}_{t=1:T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{x}^{i}_{t=1:T}</annotation></semantics></math>, is not yet dynamically feasible. Due to the recursive nature of dynamic feasibility, it is necessary to project the predicted state <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{x}^{i}_{t+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>i</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{x}^{i}_{t+1}</annotation></semantics></math> onto the <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">reachable set</span> of <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{x}^{i}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{x}^{i}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. We denote such a reachable set as <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}(\tilde{x}^{i}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}(\tilde{x}^{i}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>. We further denote <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> as a uniformly random distribution and denote the time-invariant admissible action set as <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math>. For a nonlinear system, we do not have access to the closed-form expression for such a reachable set <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math>.To address this challenge, we adopt a sampling approach: we uniformly draw a batch of <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> action samples, with each sample consisting of all degrees of freedom of the action in <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>m</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{m}</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, we obtain <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{U_{t}\}\in\mathbb{R}^{N_{p}\times m}\sim\mathcal{U}(\mathcal{A})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{U_{t}\}\in\mathbb{R}^{N_{p}\times m}\sim\mathcal{U}(\mathcal{A})</annotation></semantics></math>, where each action sample <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{U_{t}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{U_{t}\}</annotation></semantics></math> has <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> degrees of freedom. The admissible action set <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m17" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{A}:\{u^{\zeta}_{L}\leq u^{\zeta}\leq u^{\zeta}_{U}|\zeta=1,2,\dots,m\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mi>L</mi><mi>ζ</mi></msubsup><mo>≤</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mi>ζ</mi></msup><mo>≤</mo><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mi>U</mi><mi>ζ</mi></msubsup><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>ζ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}:\{u^{\zeta}_{L}\leq u^{\zeta}\leq u^{\zeta}_{U}|\zeta=1,2,\dots,m\}</annotation></semantics></math> refers to the allowable range of values between the lower bound <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{L}</annotation></semantics></math> and upper bound <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>U</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{U}</annotation></semantics></math> for each independent degree of freedom <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\zeta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ζ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, we forward propagate the <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">predicted current state</span> <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{x}^{i}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{x}^{i}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> to obtain a batch of <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">dynamically feasible samples</span> <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{x^{i}_{t+1}\}=f(\tilde{x}^{i}_{t},u_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{x^{i}_{t+1}\}=f(\tilde{x}^{i}_{t},u_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>, for all action samples <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{t}\in\{U_{t}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{t}\in\{U_{t}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Next, we replace the <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">predicted next state</span> <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{x}^{i}_{t+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>i</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{x}^{i}_{t+1}</annotation></semantics></math> with the feasible sample in <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{x^{i}_{t+1}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{x^{i}_{t+1}\}</annotation></semantics></math> that is closest to <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{x}^{i}_{t+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>i</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{x}^{i}_{t+1}</annotation></semantics></math> in terms of the 2-norm distance. In summary, we project <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{x}^{i}_{t+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>i</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{x}^{i}_{t+1}</annotation></semantics></math> onto <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}(\tilde{x}^{i}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}(\tilde{x}^{i}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> as follows:</p>
<table id="S4.E26" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{i}_{t+1}=argmin_{u_{t}}||f(\tilde{x}^{i}_{t},u_{t})-\tilde{x}^{i}_{t+1}||^{2}_{2},\forall u_{t}\in\{U_{t}\}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>i</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{i}_{t+1}=argmin_{u_{t}}||f(\tilde{x}^{i}_{t},u_{t})-\tilde{x}^{i}_{t+1}||^{2}_{2},\forall u_{t}\in\{U_{t}\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(26)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">and once we find the action <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u^{*}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u^{*}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> that minimizes the distance <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="||f(\tilde{x}^{i}_{t},u_{t})-\tilde{x}^{i}_{t+1}||^{2}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>i</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">||f(\tilde{x}^{i}_{t},u_{t})-\tilde{x}^{i}_{t+1}||^{2}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, our projected state (dynamically feasible) becomes <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{i}_{t+1}=f(\tilde{x}^{i}_{t},u^{*}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{i}_{t+1}=f(\tilde{x}^{i}_{t},u^{*}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>. This process then repeats for all <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=0,1,\dots,T-2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=0,1,\dots,T-2</annotation></semantics></math>. Our complete reverse diffusion process is formulated in Algorithm <a href="#algorithm1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ IV-A Diffusion for direct sampling of state trajectory ‣ IV CONSTRAINED DIFFUSION FOR DIRECT TRAJECTORY OPTIMIZATION ‣ PAD-TRO: Projection-Augmented Diffusion for Direct Trajectory Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.04436v1/figures/projected_trj_combined.jpg" id="S4.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/216;" width="476" height="216" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Fig. 1</span>: </span><span id="S4.F1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Front view and top-down view of open-loop trajectory generation via Alg. <a href="#algorithm1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ IV-A Diffusion for direct sampling of state trajectory ‣ IV CONSTRAINED DIFFUSION FOR DIRECT TRAJECTORY OPTIMIZATION ‣ PAD-TRO: Projection-Augmented Diffusion for Direct Trajectory Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> for a generic waypoint navigation scenario.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In addition, as the authors have noted in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>, it is only meaningful to start projecting the states when the noise level <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{i,t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{i,t}</annotation></semantics></math> is low. This is due to the fact that during the initial exploration phase with high noise, the sampled trajectories are far away from optimality. Empirically, it is also beneficial to randomly choose which time horizon <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> to project <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{x}^{i}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{x}^{i}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, for any diffusion steps <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, instead of projecting at all the time horizons. This partial projection method has the effect of minimizing the overall projection error and speeding up the projection process. We show our projection schedule in Algorithm <a href="#algorithm2" title="Algorithm 2 ‣ IV-B Dynamic feasibility via gradient-free projection ‣ IV CONSTRAINED DIFFUSION FOR DIRECT TRAJECTORY OPTIMIZATION ‣ PAD-TRO: Projection-Augmented Diffusion for Direct Trajectory Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, where we adopt a linearly interpolated projection probability between two noise thresholds <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{min}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{min}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{max}</annotation></semantics></math>, similar to <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>. Since our noise schedule <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{i,t}\in\mathbb{R}^{T\times N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{i,t}\in\mathbb{R}^{T\times N}</annotation></semantics></math> spans both the trajectory prediction horizon and the diffusion horizon, we use the average of the noise schedule computed along the trajectory prediction horizon to determine the projection threshold, i.e., <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="mean(\sigma_{i})=\frac{\sum^{k=T}_{k=1}\sigma_{i,k}}{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><msubsup><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em" stretchy="true">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msubsup></mstyle><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mi>T</mi></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">mean(\sigma_{i})=\frac{\sum^{k=T}_{k=1}\sigma_{i,k}}{T}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="algorithm2" class="ltx_float ltx_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="algorithm2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 2</span> </span>Projection Schedule Conditioned on Noise</figcaption>
<div id="algorithm2.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_lst_numbers_left ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="algorithm2.4.1" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="algorithm2.4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Input :</span> <math id="algorithm2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{i}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="algorithm2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{i}</annotation></semantics></math>
</div>
<div id="algorithm2.4.2" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm2.4.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">1</span></span>
            Choose parameters <math id="algorithm2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\max}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="algorithm2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\min}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\min}</annotation></semantics></math>;
</div>
<div id="algorithm2.4.3" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm2.4.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">2</span></span>
            <span id="algorithm2.4.3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">if</span> <em id="algorithm2.4.3.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">mean(<math id="algorithm2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{i})&gt;\sigma_{\max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">𝑂𝑃𝐸𝑁</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>max</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{i})&gt;\sigma_{\max}</annotation></semantics></math></em> <span id="algorithm2.4.3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">then</span>
</div>
<div id="algorithm2.4.4" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> 
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm2.4.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">3</span></span>
            no projection;
</div>
<div id="algorithm2.4.5" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm2.4.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">4</span></span>
            <span id="algorithm2.4.5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">else if</span> <em id="algorithm2.4.5.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic"><math id="algorithm2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\min}\leq mean(\sigma_{i})\leq\sigma_{\max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>max</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\min}\leq mean(\sigma_{i})\leq\sigma_{\max}</annotation></semantics></math></em> <span id="algorithm2.4.5.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">then</span>
</div>
<div id="algorithm2.4.6" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm2.4.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">5</span></span>
             <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> 
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm2.4.6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">6</span></span>
            Compute projection probability:

<table id="S4.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\text{proj}}=\mathrm{clip}\!\left(\tfrac{\sigma_{\max}-mean(\sigma_{i})}{\sigma_{\max}-\sigma_{\min}},0,1\right)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>proj</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.428em;" width="1.428em"><mi>clip</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>min</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\text{proj}}=\mathrm{clip}\!\left(\tfrac{\sigma_{\max}-mean(\sigma_{i})}{\sigma_{\max}-\sigma_{\min}},0,1\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="algorithm2.4.7" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm2.4.7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">7</span></span>
             <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> 
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm2.4.7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">8</span></span>
            For each timestep <math id="algorithm2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="algorithm2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{i}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="algorithm2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{i}</annotation></semantics></math>:

<ol id="S4.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S4.I1.i1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">11</span></span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Draw a random number <math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{t}\sim\mathcal{U}(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{t}\sim\mathcal{U}(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S4.I1.i2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">12</span></span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{t}&lt;p_{\text{proj}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>proj</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{t}&lt;p_{\text{proj}}</annotation></semantics></math>, project <math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{i}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{i}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{i}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{i}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S4.I1.i3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">13</span></span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Otherwise, leave <math id="S4.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{i}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{i}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S4.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{i}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{i}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> unchanged</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="algorithm2.4.8" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm2.4.8.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">9</span></span>
            <span id="algorithm2.4.8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">else</span>
</div>
<div id="algorithm2.4.9" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> 
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm2.4.9.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">10</span></span>
            always project;
</div>
<div id="algorithm2.4.10" class="ltx_listingline">
</div>
</div>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Our projection scheme differs from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite> in that our approach preserves the gradient-free nature of sampling-based trajectory optimization, whereas <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite> requires solving a convex optimization problem at each projection step.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmremark1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmremark1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 1</span></span><span id="Thmremark1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmremark1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmremark1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmremark1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Our bi-level noise schedule <math id="Thmremark1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{i,t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{i,t}</annotation></semantics></math> differs from the one used in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>. In <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>, the noise along the trajectory prediction horizon <math id="Thmremark1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=0,1,\dots,T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=0,1,\dots,T</annotation></semantics></math> increases as <math id="Thmremark1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> increases, allowing for more exploration of control inputs in the later time steps; in contrast, our algorithm diffuses states instead of control inputs. We deliberately choose the noise to decrease as <math id="Thmremark1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> increases. Intuitively, this choice adapts the bi-level noise schedule to our novel projection mechanism (see Section <a href="#S4.SS2" title="IV-B Dynamic feasibility via gradient-free projection ‣ IV CONSTRAINED DIFFUSION FOR DIRECT TRAJECTORY OPTIMIZATION ‣ PAD-TRO: Projection-Augmented Diffusion for Direct Trajectory Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">IV-B</span></a>), which only enforces dynamic feasibility when we obtain high-quality samples in the reverse diffusion process, i.e., when the noise level is low. We empirically discover that this leads to smoother trajectories.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">V </span><span id="S5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Simulation Studies</span></h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We compare our projection-augmented diffusion for direct trajectory optimization with MBD<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>, DRAX <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>, and an NLP solver, Casadi<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S5.T1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">TABLE I</span>: </span><span id="S5.T1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Performance Comparison of Open-Loop Planning</span></figcaption>
<table id="S5.T1.5" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S5.T1.5.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T1.5.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt"><span id="S5.T1.5.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Method</span></th>
<th id="S5.T1.5.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt"><span id="S5.T1.5.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Success(%)</span></th>
<th id="S5.T1.5.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="S5.T1.5.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Dist. to Goal[<math id="S5.T1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>]</span></th>
<th id="S5.T1.5.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="S5.T1.5.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Clearance[<math id="S5.T1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>]</span></th>
<th id="S5.T1.5.1.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="S5.T1.5.1.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Length[<math id="S5.T1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>]</span></th>
<th id="S5.T1.5.1.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="S5.T1.5.1.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Time[<math id="S5.T1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>]</span></th>
<th id="S5.T1.5.1.7" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="S5.T1.5.1.7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Dynamic Feasibility Error</span></th>
