<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S5.SS1.6" class="ltx_text">V-A</span> </span><span id="S5.SS1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Waypoint Navigation Scenario</span></h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In our simulation studies, we focus on a quadrotor system with the following dynamics <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>:</p>
<table id="S5.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.E27"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E27.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\dot{o}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>o</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\dot{o}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E27.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=v</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(27)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S5.E28"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle m\dot{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle m\dot{v}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E28.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=mge_{3}-FRe_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=mge_{3}-FRe_{3}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(28)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S5.E29"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E29.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\dot{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\dot{R}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E29.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=R\hat{\Omega}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=R\hat{\Omega}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(29)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S5.E30"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E30.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\Gamma\dot{\Omega}+" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover></mrow><mo>+</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\Gamma\dot{\Omega}+</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E30.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\Omega\times\Gamma\Omega=M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\Omega\times\Gamma\Omega=M</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(30)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o=[o_{x},o_{y},o_{z}]\in\mathbb{R}^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>z</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o=[o_{x},o_{y},o_{z}]\in\mathbb{R}^{3}</annotation></semantics></math> refers to the position vector, <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v=[v_{x},v_{y},v_{z}]\in\mathbb{R}^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>z</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v=[v_{x},v_{y},v_{z}]\in\mathbb{R}^{3}</annotation></semantics></math> refers to the velocity vector, <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> refers to the rotation matrix, and <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega=[\omega_{x},\omega_{y},\omega_{z}]\in\mathbb{R}^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>z</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega=[\omega_{x},\omega_{y},\omega_{z}]\in\mathbb{R}^{3}</annotation></semantics></math> refers to the angular velocity vector, <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> refers to the inertia matrix. Additionally, the control inputs are the thrust along the vertical body axis, denoted by <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math>, and the torque vector, denoted by <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=[M_{x},M_{y},M_{z}]\in\mathbb{R}^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>z</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=[M_{x},M_{y},M_{z}]\in\mathbb{R}^{3}</annotation></semantics></math>. The hat map <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\cdot}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mo>⋅</mo><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\cdot}</annotation></semantics></math> maps a vector in <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{3}</annotation></semantics></math> to a 3 by 3 skew-symmetric matrix representation. We assume the quadrotor has a mass of 1 <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="kg" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">kg</annotation></semantics></math>, and a diagonal inertial matrix with <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma_{x}</annotation></semantics></math> = 0.01 <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="kg\cdot m^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">kg\cdot m^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma_{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma_{y}</annotation></semantics></math> = 0.01 <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="kg\cdot m^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">kg\cdot m^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma_{z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>z</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma_{z}</annotation></semantics></math> = 0.02 <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="kg\cdot m^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">kg\cdot m^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. We showcase the effectiveness of our Algorithm <a href="#algorithm1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ IV-A Diffusion for direct sampling of state trajectory ‣ IV CONSTRAINED DIFFUSION FOR DIRECT TRAJECTORY OPTIMIZATION ‣ PAD-TRO: Projection-Augmented Diffusion for Direct Trajectory Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> in a waypoint navigation scenario. We assume a workspace of dimensions <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6m\times 6m\times 3m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6m\times 6m\times 3m</annotation></semantics></math>. We place 16 static cylindrical obstacles in the workspace with variable radii, randomly sampled from 0.1 meters to 0.2 meters. We assume that each cylindrical obstacle has the same height of 3.0 meters, spanning from the ground to the ceiling. By doing so, we only need to consider the horizontal distance between the quadrotor to the surface of each cylinder in the safety constraints <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> (<a href="#S4.E11" title="Equation 11 ‣ IV-A Diffusion for direct sampling of state trajectory ‣ IV CONSTRAINED DIFFUSION FOR DIRECT TRAJECTORY OPTIMIZATION ‣ PAD-TRO: Projection-Augmented Diffusion for Direct Trajectory Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We first validate Algorithm <a href="#algorithm1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ IV-A Diffusion for direct sampling of state trajectory ‣ IV CONSTRAINED DIFFUSION FOR DIRECT TRAJECTORY OPTIMIZATION ‣ PAD-TRO: Projection-Augmented Diffusion for Direct Trajectory Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> on a <span id="S5.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">single test case</span>, then we comprehensively compare our approach with baselines over <span id="S5.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">100 randomized trials</span> (see Section <a href="#S5.SS2" title="V-B Comparison Against Baselines ‣ V Simulation Studies ‣ PAD-TRO: Projection-Augmented Diffusion for Direct Trajectory Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">V-B</span></a>). In the single test case, we assume the initial position is given by <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[-1,-1,0.5]\in\mathbb{R}^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[-1,-1,0.5]\in\mathbb{R}^{3}</annotation></semantics></math> and the goal position is given by <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[1,1,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[1,1,1]</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\in\mathbb{R}^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\in\mathbb{R}^{3}</annotation></semantics></math>. All positional units are measured in meters. We use a trajectory prediction horizon of <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> = 50, a sampling interval of <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="dt" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">dt</annotation></semantics></math> = 0.1 <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>, a diffusion horizon of <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> = 200, a temperature <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> = 0.1 in <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{J}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>J</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{J}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa=5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa=5</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{g}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{g}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math>. We apply the Runge-Kutta 4 (RK4) discretization method to simulate the dynamics. We use <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> = 256 samples for the batch sampling step in Algorithm <a href="#algorithm1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ IV-A Diffusion for direct sampling of state trajectory ‣ IV CONSTRAINED DIFFUSION FOR DIRECT TRAJECTORY OPTIMIZATION ‣ PAD-TRO: Projection-Augmented Diffusion for Direct Trajectory Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. For the projection mechanism, we use <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> = 250 samples, <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{max}=0.3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{max}=0.3</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{min}=0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{min}=0.1</annotation></semantics></math>. For our noise schedule (<a href="#S4.E10" title="Equation 10 ‣ IV-A Diffusion for direct sampling of state trajectory ‣ IV CONSTRAINED DIFFUSION FOR DIRECT TRAJECTORY OPTIMIZATION ‣ PAD-TRO: Projection-Augmented Diffusion for Direct Trajectory Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>), we use <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> = <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\times 10^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\times 10^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{N}=1\times 10^{-2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{N}=1\times 10^{-2}</annotation></semantics></math>, which determines <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math>. We also choose <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta=0.8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta=0.8</annotation></semantics></math> (<a href="#S4.E10" title="Equation 10 ‣ IV-A Diffusion for direct sampling of state trajectory ‣ IV CONSTRAINED DIFFUSION FOR DIRECT TRAJECTORY OPTIMIZATION ‣ PAD-TRO: Projection-Augmented Diffusion for Direct Trajectory Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>), which controls the noise level over the trajectory prediction horizon. We show the open-loop trajectory in Figure <a href="#S4.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ IV-B Dynamic feasibility via gradient-free projection ‣ IV CONSTRAINED DIFFUSION FOR DIRECT TRAJECTORY OPTIMIZATION ‣ PAD-TRO: Projection-Augmented Diffusion for Direct Trajectory Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.04436v1/figures/combined_trj_all_methods.jpg" id="S5.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/216;" width="476" height="216" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Fig. 2</span>: </span><span id="S5.F2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Comparison of open-loop trajectories generated by our method and the baselines. All trajectories shown here are collision-free.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S5.SS2.6" class="ltx_text">V-B</span> </span><span id="S5.SS2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Comparison Against Baselines</span></h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We comprehensively validate our approach in open-loop trajectory by comparing it to three baselines: MBD <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>, DRAX <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>, and NLP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite> in the same workspace defined in Section <a href="#S5.SS1" title="V-A Waypoint Navigation Scenario ‣ V Simulation Studies ‣ PAD-TRO: Projection-Augmented Diffusion for Direct Trajectory Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">V-A</span></a>. In each simulation trial, the initial and goal position pairs are randomly generated outside the obstacles and are at least 0.1 <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> away from the surface of each obstacle. To be consistent with MBD <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite> and DRAX <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>, our implementation also relies on the JAX package <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>, which enables GPU accelerated parallel computations in the forward propagation of the dynamics. All simulations are performed on a PC with an NVIDIA RTX 4080 GPU with 16GB of RAM.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate the performance of open-loop trajectory planning. We present the statistics collected from 100 randomized trials for open-loop trajectory optimization in Table <a href="#S5.T1" title="Table I ‣ V Simulation Studies ‣ PAD-TRO: Projection-Augmented Diffusion for Direct Trajectory Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">I</span></a>. We define the success rate as the percentage of trials where no collision occurs. To evaluate the dynamic feasibility of our Algorithm <a href="#algorithm1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ IV-A Diffusion for direct sampling of state trajectory ‣ IV CONSTRAINED DIFFUSION FOR DIRECT TRAJECTORY OPTIMIZATION ‣ PAD-TRO: Projection-Augmented Diffusion for Direct Trajectory Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> after the diffusion process is complete at <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1</annotation></semantics></math> (<a href="#S4.E15" title="Equation 15 ‣ IV-A Diffusion for direct sampling of state trajectory ‣ IV CONSTRAINED DIFFUSION FOR DIRECT TRAJECTORY OPTIMIZATION ‣ PAD-TRO: Projection-Augmented Diffusion for Direct Trajectory Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a>), we apply the control input <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> that is chosen during projection (<a href="#S4.E26" title="Equation 26 ‣ IV-B Dynamic feasibility via gradient-free projection ‣ IV CONSTRAINED DIFFUSION FOR DIRECT TRAJECTORY OPTIMIZATION ‣ PAD-TRO: Projection-Augmented Diffusion for Direct Trajectory Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">26</span></a>). To evaluate the dynamic feasibility of DRAX <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>, we apply the control inputs returned by their algorithm. We drop the <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\cdot}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mo>⋅</mo><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\cdot}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{0}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{0}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> here since the reverse diffusion process is complete, and our projection mechanism is applied to all states along the trajectory prediction horizon <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> towards the end of the reverse diffusion process (see Algorithm <a href="#algorithm2" title="Algorithm 2 ‣ IV-B Dynamic feasibility via gradient-free projection ‣ IV CONSTRAINED DIFFUSION FOR DIRECT TRAJECTORY OPTIMIZATION ‣ PAD-TRO: Projection-Augmented Diffusion for Direct Trajectory Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>). We define the trajectory-wise dynamic feasibility error as:</p>
<table id="S5.E31" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E31.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="err_{f}=\frac{\sum^{T-1}_{t=0}||f(x_{t},u_{t})-x_{t+1}||^{2}_{2}}{T}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><msubsup><mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mi>T</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">err_{f}=\frac{\sum^{T-1}_{t=0}||f(x_{t},u_{t})-x_{t+1}||^{2}_{2}}{T}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(31)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We define the success of open-loop planning as a trajectory that leads to no collision with the boundary of the workspace or the obstacles. From Table <a href="#S5.T1" title="Table I ‣ V Simulation Studies ‣ PAD-TRO: Projection-Augmented Diffusion for Direct Trajectory Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">I</span></a>, we observe that in open-loop trajectory planning, our method outperforms all baselines in terms of the success rate, distance to goal, and trajectory length. However, the robustness of our method comes at the cost of extra computation time compared to the other baselines. While DRAX <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite> also applies direct trajectory optimization via a diffusion-style process, it results in a high dynamic feasibility error, whereas our approach leads to <span id="S5.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">zero dynamic feasibility error</span>. In addition, our method leads to approximately <span id="S5.SS2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">4x higher success rate</span> compared to DRAX <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>. We note that the average clearance from obstacles for DRAX <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite> is negative, indicating that it is highly prone to collisions<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
              
              
              
              
              
              
              
            Clearance distance is positive if a trajectory lies outside the surface of the obstacles; otherwise, it is negative.</span></span></span>. While MBD <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite> yields a success rate of 68% in open-loop planning, it leads to sub-optimal trajectories as reflected by the trajectory length and the distance to goal error, i.e., the distance between the position at the last time step <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> of the open-loop trajectory and the designated goal position. We observe that NLP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite> leads to a 25% lower success rate than our Algorithm <a href="#algorithm1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ IV-A Diffusion for direct sampling of state trajectory ‣ IV CONSTRAINED DIFFUSION FOR DIRECT TRAJECTORY OPTIMIZATION ‣ PAD-TRO: Projection-Augmented Diffusion for Direct Trajectory Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, indicating its lack of robustness.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Regarding the computation time, when using the same number of diffusion steps (<math id="S5.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>=200), we observe that DRAX <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite> outperforms our method at the cost of a <span id="S5.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">3x lower</span> success rate and non-zero dynamic feasibility error <math id="S5.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="err_{f}=3.3\pm 2.3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>3.3</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>2.3</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">err_{f}=3.3\pm 2.3</annotation></semantics></math>. In addition, when we double the number of diffusion steps for DRAX (<math id="S5.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>=400), the success rate barely improves. In addition, this leads to an even higher dynamic feasibility error overall. Intuitively, DRAX <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite> is unable to sample from the true target distribution of trajectories that account for both optimality and dynamic feasibility. We also note that NLP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>, MBD <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>, and DRAX <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite> are all faster than our approach. While DRAX <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite> is twice as fast as our approach, we find that MBD <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite> is comparable to ours. The relatively slow computation speed is attributed to our projection mechanism, which cannot be parallelized due to its chronological nature. Further study is required to investigate how we can speed up our projection-augmented diffusion framework. Finally, we show a visual comparison from one of the simulation trials between the trajectories generated by our method and the baselines in Figure <a href="#S5.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ V-A Waypoint Navigation Scenario ‣ V Simulation Studies ‣ PAD-TRO: Projection-Augmented Diffusion for Direct Trajectory Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. We observe that MBD <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite> misses the goal position and almost collides with an obstacle. Trajectories generated by DRAX <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>, NLP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>, and our approach all reach the goal position. For NLP, the trajectory initially overshoots the goal position before reaching it, indicating sub-optimality. Our approach leads to a trajectory that has more curvature than DRAX <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite> since there is no dynamic feasibility violation.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">VI </span><span id="S6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Conclusion</span></h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We have proposed a novel direct trajectory optimization framework via model-based diffusion. We have introduced a novel gradient-free projection mechanism for ensuring dynamic feasibility. To balance exploration and optimality, we have adopted a novel bi-level noise schedule on both the diffusion horizon and the trajectory prediction horizon. Our results show that our method outperforms baselines in various metrics in a quadrotor navigation problem in a cluttered environment. The projection framework in our approach relies on pre-set noise thresholds. Further investigation is required to study how to design an adaptive and potentially more efficient projection mechanism that is dynamics-aware. In addition, accelerating the projection-augmented diffusion process for faster convergence is a potential research direction. Last but not least, further validation in hardware experiments on complex robotic systems, such as quadruped robots, is needed.</p>
</div>
</section>
